WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.470
Geologists record field sketches, photographs, rock samples, strike and dip measurements,

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:07.670
and the positions of contacts and structures.

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:11.650
Measurements let them project features below the surface and connect separated

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:14.550
outcrops. Record the observation before the interpretation.

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.350
A fault is younger than a unit it cuts.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:21.470
An unfaulted layer or intrusion that seals the fault is younger

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.730
than the movement it seals at that exposure.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:25.910
These relations bracket an interval;

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:29.250
they do not prove that a fault never moved elsewhere or

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:29.910
at another time.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.710
Minerals may grow before, during, or after deformation.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:37.390
Their shapes and positions relative to cleavage and veins can help

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:42.650
establish a sequence. Microscopic textures and mineral chemistry add evidence about

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:43.990
metamorphic conditions.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:50.290
Folded veins, later cleavage, and cross-cutting features can record multiple events.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:54.050
Distinguish an observed overprint from the proposed explanation.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.890
Missing exposures and uncertain ages can limit the sequence to relative

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:00.090
order rather than a precise date.
